The global map depicts results that are not simply binary. Some choices reflect old alliances: Japan and Australia with Washington, Russia and Pakistan with Beijing. Others reflect what lies underground. Chile, sitting on the world's largest copper output and lithium reserves, has joined Pax Silica;

Indonesia, the dominant source of nickel, sits with WAICO. A handful of states, Kazakhstan chief among them, have simply joined both, unwilling to trade dependence on one great power for dependence on another. Still others, from Vietnam to Saudi Arabia, remain on the sidelines, weighing which hand is worth playing.

Canada and Mexico remain wild cards. Which alliance they join in the AI race for minerals and other resources will likely depend on the trade "negotiations" with Washington.

